  <article class="blog-post">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-post-header">
        <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back">&larr; Back to Blog</a>
        <span class="blog-tag chip chip--blue">O-Level English</span>
        <h1>O-Level Situational Writing: Marks, Planning and Format</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-09-23">Updated 23 September 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">9 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>Situational writing is Section B of O-Level English Paper 1 (syllabus 1184). You are given a situation and a visual text, such as a poster, flyer or programme, and you write a letter, email, report, speech or similar text of <strong>250 to 350 words</strong>. It carries <strong>30 marks</strong>. Plan to spend 30 to 35 minutes of the 1 hour 50 minutes allowed for the paper.</p>
        <p>This guide covers how the marks work, how to plan, how to develop each point and the format each text type expects. It follows the two reference documents we teach from in class: our Situational Writing Blueprint and our Situational Writing Content Bank. Learn the routine once and you can use it on every paper.</p>

        <h2>How the 30 marks are awarded</h2>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Marks</th><th>What the examiner assesses</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Criterion">Task Fulfilment</td><td data-label="Marks">10</td><td data-label="What the examiner assesses">Whether every required point is addressed and developed; awareness of purpose, audience and context; use of the given information</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Criterion">Language</td><td data-label="Marks">20</td><td data-label="What the examiner assesses">Organisation of ideas, clarity of expression, and accuracy of grammar, vocabulary, spelling and punctuation</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Three facts follow from this table.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Language is worth twice as much as Task Fulfilment.</strong> Covering every point won&rsquo;t save a response written in flat, repetitive sentences.</li>
          <li><strong>Missing one content point caps Task Fulfilment at Band 3</strong>, a maximum of 6 out of 10, however well the rest is written. Under syllabus 1184, Band 5 is the highest band.</li>
          <li><strong>There is no separate format mark.</strong> Format counts as evidence that you understand the purpose, audience and context, which is assessed under Task Fulfilment. A missing address does not cost a fixed number of marks, and a perfect layout earns nothing if content points are missing.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Before you write: the PAC analysis</h2>
        <p>Every decision in Section B comes from three questions. Answer them in the margin before you draft.</p>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Question"><strong>P</strong>urpose: why am I writing?</td><td data-label="Where to find the answer">The verb in the instruction: persuade, inform, request, complain, propose, invite, report</td><td data-label="What it decides">What the closing paragraph must do, and whether the text argues, explains or narrates</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Question"><strong>A</strong>udience: who reads this?</td><td data-label="Where to find the answer">The named recipient and their relationship to the writer</td><td data-label="What it decides">Register, salutation, sign-off, and which benefits are worth mentioning</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Question"><strong>C</strong>ontext: what has happened, and what is my standing?</td><td data-label="Where to find the answer">The situation paragraph and the visual text</td><td data-label="What it decides">The opening sentence and the tone: grateful, concerned, apologetic or assertive</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Then write one word at the top of your plan: <strong>formal</strong>, <strong>semi-formal</strong> or <strong>informal</strong>. Check every salutation, contraction, word choice and sign-off against it. An inconsistent register is penalised more heavily than one that is cautious but consistent.</p>

        <h2>Count the content points correctly</h2>
        <p>Candidates often count the bullets printed on the paper instead of the instructions inside them. A bullet such as &ldquo;suggest one activity and explain why it would appeal to students&rdquo; contains two points: the activity and the reason. Answer only the first and you have addressed &ldquo;most&rdquo; points, which caps Task Fulfilment at Band 3.</p>
        <p>Apply three checks to every bullet:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Count the verbs.</strong> Two instructions, such as &ldquo;suggest and explain&rdquo;, almost always mean two points.</li>
          <li><strong>Count the audiences or subjects.</strong> &ldquo;Benefits to mentors and mentees&rdquo; names two groups, so two points.</li>
          <li><strong>Count the numbers.</strong> &ldquo;Two improvements&rdquo; sets two points, each developed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Renumber the points in the margin after splitting them, and plan one paragraph for each.</p>

        <h2>The five-step routine</h2>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Time</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Step">1. Read and analyse</td><td data-label="Time">3&ndash;4 min</td><td data-label="What to do">Mark the text type, the role you write as, the named audience and every content point. Write the PAC and the register word.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Step">2. Annotate the visual text</td><td data-label="Time">with step 1</td><td data-label="What to do">Number each usable detail 1, 2 or 3 according to the point it serves. Strike through everything unnumbered: the visual text always contains more than you need.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Step">3. Plan</td><td data-label="Time">3&ndash;4 min</td><td data-label="What to do">Five paragraphs: opening, one per content point, closing. Beside each point, note how you will develop it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Step">4. Write</td><td data-label="Time">18&ndash;20 min</td><td data-label="What to do">State your purpose in the first sentence of the body. Give each point its own paragraph of 60 to 80 words.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Step">5. Check tone and proofread</td><td data-label="Time">4&ndash;5 min</td><td data-label="What to do">Confirm the register is consistent. Read from the last sentence backwards for agreement, tense, punctuation and spelling. Cross off every numbered point.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Aim for 300 to 320 words: an opening of 40 to 50 words, three body paragraphs of 70 to 80 words, and a closing of 40 to 50 words. Writing well under 250 words almost always means the points have not been developed. Writing well over 350 does not raise the mark and takes time from Continuous Writing, which carries the same 30 marks.</p>

        <h2>Develop every point: the four-move expansion</h2>
        <p>The commonest reason for a capped mark is a response that mentions every bullet but develops none of them. Build each body paragraph from four moves.</p>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Example (formal email to a principal)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Move"><strong>State</strong></td><td data-label="Question it answers">What is the point, in one clear sentence?</td><td data-label="Example (formal email to a principal)">The congestion in the canteen during the first break has become a serious difficulty for the lower secondary levels.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Move"><strong>Specify</strong></td><td data-label="Question it answers">Which detail makes it concrete: a time, a number, a place, a name?</td><td data-label="Example (formal email to a principal)">With all six levels released at 9.45 a.m., the queues regularly extend past the notice board, and Secondary One students frequently reach the front with fewer than five minutes remaining.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Move"><strong>Show</strong></td><td data-label="Question it answers">What follows from it?</td><td data-label="Example (formal email to a principal)">Many of them therefore return to class without eating, and several have reported feeling unwell during the fourth period.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Move"><strong>Sell</strong></td><td data-label="Question it answers">Why should this reader care?</td><td data-label="Example (formal email to a principal)">Staggering the break would remove this problem at no cost to the school and would reduce the disruption that late arrivals currently cause to lessons.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Most candidates manage State and Specify. Show and Sell reach the upper bands, because they show awareness of the reader. A principal is persuaded by order, safety and cost; a friend by enjoyment and a shared experience. The same fact must be sold differently to each.</p>
        <p>Some students prefer PEEL (Point, Evidence, Explanation, Link); both methods produce the development Bands 4 and 5 require.</p>
        <p>Use the visual text, but never copy it. Turn each detail into a reason, a consequence or a benefit that matters to the named reader. Lifting a phrase word for word shows the examiner nothing about your language.</p>

        <h2>Format for each text type</h2>
        <p>The syllabus names four text types: email, letter, report and speech. School examinations also set proposals and articles, and schools distinguish formal from informal letters. Each has a short checklist in our Blueprint.</p>
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead><tr><th>Text type</th><th>Checklist</th><th>Opening and close</th><th>Format essentials</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Formal letter</td><td data-label="Checklist">CHOPE IT</td><td data-label="Opening and close">&ldquo;Dear Mr/Mrs/Ms [Surname]&rdquo; with &ldquo;Yours sincerely&rdquo;; &ldquo;Dear Sir/Madam&rdquo; with &ldquo;Yours faithfully&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Format essentials">Sender&rsquo;s address top right; date in full; recipient&rsquo;s address left-aligned; underlined subject line</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Informal letter</td><td data-label="Checklist">SHIOK</td><td data-label="Opening and close">&ldquo;Dear [first name]&rdquo; or &ldquo;Hi [first name]&rdquo;; &ldquo;Take care&rdquo; or &ldquo;Best wishes&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Format essentials">Sender&rsquo;s address and date; no recipient&rsquo;s address; warm but fully developed</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Email</td><td data-label="Checklist">STAMP</td><td data-label="Opening and close">Formal: &ldquo;Dear Mr/Mrs [Name]&rdquo; and &ldquo;Regards&rdquo;. Informal: &ldquo;Hi [Name]&rdquo; and &ldquo;Cheers&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Format essentials">To, From, Date and a specific Subject line; no postal addresses</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Report</td><td data-label="Checklist">TIKAM</td><td data-label="Opening and close">No salutation or sign-off; ends with a conclusion</td><td data-label="Format essentials">Title; Prepared by; Submitted to; date; numbered section headings; recommendations tied to the findings</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Speech</td><td data-label="Checklist">KENA</td><td data-label="Opening and close">&ldquo;Good morning, teachers and fellow students&rdquo;; ends with a call to action and &ldquo;Thank you&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Format essentials">No addresses, date or sign-off; a hook, direct address and rhetorical devices</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Proposal</td><td data-label="Checklist">BOLEH</td><td data-label="Opening and close">No salutation or sign-off; ends with a conclusion</td><td data-label="Format essentials">Title; Prepared by; Submitted to; date; numbered headings; a section answering likely objections</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Text type">Article</td><td data-label="Checklist">CLICK</td><td data-label="Opening and close">No salutation or sign-off; ends with a memorable closing line</td><td data-label="Format essentials">Headline; byline; an opening hook; semi-formal register</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Never write a checklist name into your answer.</strong> The acronyms are planning aids for the margin: examiners don&rsquo;t recognise them, and none earns a mark. The email checklist STAMP is unrelated to STAMP CARD, the framework we teach for the editing section.</p>

        <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Treating format as the main score.</strong> Layout is evidence of purpose, audience and context, not a separate mark. The points carry the credit.</li>
          <li><strong>Writing one sentence per content point.</strong> Each point needs two to three sentences of development after the opening statement.</li>
          <li><strong>Burying the purpose.</strong> State it in the first sentence of the body, whatever the text type.</li>
          <li><strong>Mixing registers.</strong> No contractions in a formal letter; no &ldquo;Yours sincerely&rdquo; to a friend.</li>
          <li><strong>Inventing points the question did not ask for.</strong> They use words and earn nothing.</li>
          <li><strong>Using your own name.</strong> Sign with the name given in the question.</li>
          <li><strong>Putting postal addresses on an email, or &ldquo;Dear&rdquo; on a speech.</strong> Both show a memorised letter format rather than awareness of the text type.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>The SHARP Method for situational writing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>See:</strong> the PAC analysis, the register word and the corrected count of content points.</li>
          <li><strong>Hit:</strong> the format checklist for the text type, and the four-move expansion for every point.</li>
          <li><strong>Apply:</strong> five paragraphs inside the 300&ndash;320 word budget, with each visual-text detail converted into a reason or benefit.</li>
          <li><strong>Refine:</strong> check the tone, proofread backwards and cross off every numbered point.</li>
          <li><strong>Practise:</strong> timed responses across all seven text types, marked against the band descriptors.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In class, your child gets written, marker-style feedback on their work every week, judged against those band descriptors. They see exactly which point they missed or which move they skipped, and they fix it next time.</p>
      </div>
